    What was actually built in B1:

    - A machine-verifiable baseline: 14 functional domains / 99 modules,
      five statuses (ahead / aligned / partial / gap / not_applicable).
      Every non-not_applicable module MUST point at evidence paths that
      really exist in this repository (evidence.verify_evidence()).
      Every `ahead` module MUST carry an ahead_reason and belong to one
      of six superiority dimensions. Every `gap` MUST be assigned to a
      wave. Derived-consistency checks prevent inflating the numbers by
      editing status definitions.
    - Six RUNTIME probes (superiority.py) that actually call existing
      code rather than quoting docs: injecting a non-existent hook binary,
      tampering an HMAC record, feeding malformed config, corrupting an
      MCP policy file to check locked state, counting real i18n/README
      files, and validating the 165-module Codex TUI parity matrix.
      A dimension whose probe fails is reported as UNVERIFIED and its
      `ahead` modules are NOT counted as verified.
    - `fully_superior` has a HARD criterion written in matrix.py:
      gaps == 0 AND all six probes OK AND every dimension has a verified
      ahead module AND all B-waves done AND an external adaptive red
      team has signed off. It is currently FALSE (30 gaps, red team not
      done) and MUST NOT be reached by relaxing the criterion.
    - `tuan claude parity` / --gaps / --probes / --report-json.

    B1 production-code changes (two real fail-open side doors):

      1. `hooks/manager.py`: all four hook executors (command / http /
         prompt / subagent) previously returned None on spawn failure,
         timeout, communicate error, HTTP error, missing kernel/model or
         nonzero exit; `run_pre()` treated None as "continue", i.e. a
         broken safety hook silently ALLOWED the tool call. Now failures
         are recorded as structured `HookFailure` entries (queryable via
         `failures()`), and `hooks.on_failure` DEFAULTS TO "block" for
         PreToolUse with a machine-readable `HookDecision.failure_kind`
         and an audit trail. `ask` also blocks with a manual-confirmation
         marker; `allow` is an explicit, audited escape hatch. Unknown
         values fall back to the strictest policy. Watchdog-thread
         timeouts are recorded on the MAIN thread because last_failure
         is thread-local (otherwise fail-closed could be bypassed).
         Notification-type events record failures but never stall the
         turn (they have no action to block).
      2. `hooks/manager.py`: `blocking` default changed False -> True.
         Previously a hook returning {"block": true} was silently
         swallowed unless the user explicitly wrote `blocking: true`.
         `blocking` governs ONLY a hook's own block decision; it does
         NOT govern execution-failure fail-closed.
      3. `config/security_schema.py`: validates `hooks.on_failure`
         enum plus per-hook overrides; explicit `allow` is surfaced as a
         security-downgrade warning.

    Behavioral change (disclosed, with migration): a hook that used to
    "fail open" now blocks by default. `hooks.on_failure: allow`
    (global or per hook) restores the old behavior and emits a
    `failure_allowed` audit event. The pre-existing test
    `test_timeout_kills_hook` (which asserted timeout must NOT block) was
    updated to assert fail-closed, and a new
    `test_timeout_can_be_explicitly_allowed` proves the escape hatch
    stays reachable. We did not relax a policy merely to turn a test
    green.

